## Claim
Text: "Ulysses S. Grant, commander of the Union Army during the American Civil War, was a slave owner."
Claim author: Darryl Wayne
Claim date: October 10, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20200614150518/https://www.facebook.com/photo.php?fbid=942597979544466

## Initial Q&A
### Did Ulysses S. Grant hold the formal military title or position of Commanding General of the Union Army during the American Civil War?
Answer: Yes, Ulysses S. Grant held the formal title of Commanding General of the Union Army during the American Civil War. After his victory at Chattanooga, "President Lincoln promoted Grant to a special regular army rank, General-in-chief (Lieutenant General), authorized by Congress on March 2, 1864," giving him charge of all Union armies.

Source URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulysses_S._Grant_and_the_American_Civil_War

### Did Ulysses S. Grant legally own any enslaved individuals at any point in his life?
Answer: Yes, Ulysses S. Grant legally owned one enslaved individual, a man named William Jones, in the late 1850s, which is documented in the 1859 manumission record where Grant freed him.

Source URL: https://www.clevelandcivilwarroundtable.com/the-last-u-s-president-who-was-a-slaveholder/

### If Ulysses S. Grant owned enslaved people, what were the names of those individuals and what is the historical evidence for this ownership?
Answer: According to the historical record, the only enslaved individual known to have been legally owned by Ulysses S. Grant was a man named **William Jones**. The evidence for this ownership is a manumission document Grant signed in 1859, which states Jones was “purchased by me [Grant] of Frederick Dent.”

Source URL: https://www.whitehousehistory.org/bios/ulysses-s-grant

### During what specific period of his life (e.g., pre-Civil War, during the Civil War) was Ulysses S. Grant documented as a slave owner?
Answer: Ulysses S. Grant was documented as a slave owner during the **pre-Civil War period, specifically from 1854 to 1859**, while he was living as a farmer at White Haven, an 850-acre plantation near St. Louis, Missouri. As the article states, "From 1854 to 1859, Grant struggled to support his family as a hardscrabble farmer in St. Louis, Missouri... Enslaved labor did most of the work at White Haven, and at some point Grant acquired ownership of William Jones." He then freed Jones by signing a manumission paper on **March 29, 1859**, making Jones "the last enslaved person ever owned by a U.S. president" and Grant "the last of twelve presidents in U.S. history to have been a slaveholder."

Source URL: https://www.journalofthecivilwarera.org/2018/12/the-mystery-of-william-jones-an-enslaved-man-owned-by-ulysses-s-grant/

### Did Ulysses S. Grant ever voluntarily free any enslaved person he owned, and if so, what are the details of that manumission?
Answer: Yes, Ulysses S. Grant voluntarily freed an enslaved man named William Jones in March 1859. According to the source, Grant either purchased or was given Jones (aged about 35) in 1859, and the man was in Grant's service for about a year before the Civil War began. Notably, rather than selling Jones or arranging a purchase of his own freedom, Grant simply freed him outright. The manumission document filed in St. Louis reads: "I Ulysses S Grant…for diverse good and valuable considerations me hereunto moving, do hereby emancipate and set free from Slavery my negro man William, sometimes called William Jones (Jones) of Mullatto complexion, aged about thirty-five years, and about five feet seven inches in height and being the same slave purchased by me of Frederick Dent—And I do hereby manumit, emancipate & set free said William from slavery forever." The source emphasizes that "it is notable that Grant did not sell or work out a plan with Jones to purchase his freedom, but simply freed him."

Source URL: https://acwm.org/blog/myths-misunderstandings-grant-slaveholder/

### What was the connection between Ulysses S. Grant's wife, Julia Dent Grant, and the institution of slavery, and how did that relationship affect Grant?
Answer: Ulysses S. Grant's wife, Julia Dent Grant, was raised on a Missouri plantation that used enslaved labor, and she herself was a slave mistress; her family's wealth and lifestyle were built on slavery. This relationship directly drew Grant into the institution after their marriage, as he managed her father's enslaved workers and at one point personally owned an enslaved man named William Jones, whom he later emancipated. The search result states, "Grant himself came into ownership of a man named William Jones from his father-in-law at some point during the 1850s," confirming his direct participation in slavery through this familial connection.

Source URL: https://www.whitehousehistory.org/the-formerly-enslaved-household-of-the-grant-family

### What historical sources (e.g., letters, biographies, census records) provide evidence for or against Ulysses S. Grant's ownership of enslaved people?
Answer: Historical sources that provide evidence regarding Ulysses S. Grant's ownership of enslaved people include the 1859 manumission document he signed freeing William Jones, which states Jones was "purchased by me of Frederick Dent," as well as plantation records and letters from his time managing his father-in-law's Missouri estate. The provided source notes that "there are no known documents or letters related to a bill of sale" for other individuals, but confirms Grant's direct ownership of William Jones and his role overseeing enslaved labor.

Source URL: https://www.whitehousehistory.org/bios/ulysses-s-grant

### Did Ulysses S. Grant's views on slavery and abolition evolve over the course of his lifetime, particularly following the Civil War?
Answer: Yes, Ulysses S. Grant's views on slavery evolved significantly over his lifetime. While initially ambivalent and having briefly owned an enslaved man, his views shifted during the Civil War as he came to see slavery as morally indefensible and a direct cause of the conflict; after the war, as President, he became a strong advocate for the rights of freed slaves.

Source URL: https://acwm.org/blog/myths-misunderstandings-grant-slaveholder/

### How did Ulysses S. Grant's personal history with slave ownership reconcile with his role as the military leader who ultimately helped defeat the Confederacy and end slavery?
Answer: While Ulysses S. Grant did own one enslaved person, William Jones, whom he freed in 1859 despite financial hardship, his later actions as the commanding general of the Union Army and as President demonstrated a decisive alignment against slavery and its legacy. His military strategy directly led to the defeat of the Confederacy, and as President, he "sent federal troops to the South to enforce civil rights legislation and protect African Americans from civil and political violence," actively suppressing the Ku Klux Klan.

Source URL: https://www.whitehousehistory.org/bios/ulysses-s-grant

### Do reputable historians and historical organizations broadly agree that Ulysses S. Grant was, at some point, a slave owner?
Answer: Yes, reputable historians and historical organizations broadly agree that Ulysses S. Grant was, at some point, a slave owner. The American Civil War Museum notes that "Grant did in fact own a man named William Jones for about a year on the eve of the Civil War," and that he later freed him in 1859, providing a manumission document as evidence.

Source URL: https://acwm.org/blog/myths-misunderstandings-grant-slaveholder/

## Final Judgement
The fact-check conclusively establishes that Ulysses S. Grant held the title of Commanding General of the Union Army during the American Civil War. More importantly, it provides clear historical evidence, primarily a signed manumission document from 1859, that Grant legally owned one enslaved person named William Jones during the 1850s. Reputable historical sources corroborate this fact, and there is no evidence contradicting this specific ownership.

The evidence is sufficient and unambiguous, allowing for a clear decision.

The claim states that Ulysses S. Grant, who was indeed the commander of the Union Army during the Civil War, "was a slave owner." The documented evidence from the fact-check directly and explicitly confirms that Grant legally owned at least one enslaved person for a period in his life. Therefore, the claim is `supported`.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
Historical evidence, including the [1859 manumission document](https://acwm.org/blog/myths-misunderstandings-grant-slaveholder/) Grant himself signed, confirms that he legally owned one enslaved man, **William Jones**, during the pre–Civil War period (approximately 1854–1859) while farming at White Haven, a plantation near St. Louis connected to his wife Julia Dent Grant's family, who were [slaveholders](https://www.whitehousehistory.org/the-formerly-enslaved-household-of-the-grant-family). Grant freed Jones in March 1859, notably choosing to emancipate him outright rather than sell him, even amid [financial hardship](https://www.journalofthecivilwarera.org/2018/12/the-mystery-of-william-jones-an-enslaved-man-owned-by-ulysses-s-grant/). While no other bill of sale for additional enslaved individuals has been found, reputable sources such as the [American Civil War Museum](https://acwm.org/blog/myths-misunderstandings-grant-slaveholder/) and the [White House Historical Association](https://www.whitehousehistory.org/bios/ulysses-s-grant) corroborate this ownership, and historians broadly agree that Grant was indeed a slave owner at one point in his life—making him the [last U.S. president to have held enslaved people](https://www.clevelandcivilwarroundtable.com/the-last-u-s-president-who-was-a-slaveholder/).